  "content": {
    "title": "SpaceX plans Starlink missions in CA. Vandenberg rocket launch schedule",
    "text": "March 16, 2026, 4:30 a.m. PTSpaceX plans to launch two Falcon 9 rockets from the Vandenberg Space Force Base in the next week.Each mission will deploy a batch of Starlink broadband internet satellites into low-Earth orbit.The upcoming launches are scheduled for March 16 and March 20.Four rockets have launched so far in March from California, and two more could launch within the next week.SpaceX, the commercial spaceflight company founded by billionaire Elon Musk, has plans to deploy two batches of its Starlink broadband internet satellites into low-Earth orbit. And, unsurprisingly, the spacecraft that will be making each delivery from the Vandenberg Space Force Base is SpaceX's 230-foot Falcon 9 rocket.Californians – and potentially Arizonans – making their plans to watch any launches should keep in mind that lots can change between now and when each rocket is expected to lift off, as delays in spaceflight are common. Be sure to visit VC Star for the latest mission updates.In the meantime, here's your weekly look at the upcoming launch schedule at the Vandenberg Space Force Base in Santa Barbara County.Monday, March 16: Starlink 17-24Agency: SpaceXMission: Deploy 25 Starlink satellites into low-Earth orbit, according to SpaceXRocket: Falcon 9, a 230-foot, two-stage reusable rocket capable of carrying more than 50,000 pounds of cargo to low-Earth orbitLaunch window: 7:37 to 11:37 p.m. PT Monday, March 16, 2026Rocket launch location: Space Launch Complex 4E from Vandenberg Space Force Base in CaliforniaBooster landing: SpaceX drone ship, nicknamed \"Of Course I Still Love You,\" in the Pacific OceanFriday, March 20: Starlink 17-15Agency: SpaceXMission: Deploy 25 Starlink satellites into low-Earth orbit, according to SpaceXRocket: Falcon 9, a 230-foot, two-stage reusable rocket capable of carrying more than 50,000 pounds of cargo to low-Earth orbitLaunch window: 2:48 to 6:48 p.m. PT Friday, March 20, 2026Rocket launch location: Space Launch Complex 4E from Vandenberg Space Force Base in CaliforniaBooster landing: SpaceX drone ship, nicknamed \"Of Course I Still Love You,\" in the Pacific OceanEric Lagatta is the Space Connect reporter for the USA TODAY Network. Reach him at elagatta@usatodayco.com",
    "word_count": 338,
